From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: herdr maintainers <maintainers@herdr.dev>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] expose ConPTY handles for cross-process hand-off

Tanuki's self-update flow needs to replace the running tanuki server
process without killing the shells running under it. On Windows, the
PTY backend is ConPTY via portable-pty, whose HPCON/pipe handles are
not reachable outside the crate -- MasterPty's trait object only
exposes Read/Write/resize, never the raw kernel handles a successor
process would need to DuplicateHandle in and keep the session alive.

Adds PsuedoCon::{handoff_handles,into_handoff_handles,from_handoff_handles}
and ConPtyMasterPty::into_handoff / master_from_handoff, reaching past
the opaque HPCON to the undocumented _PseudoConsole{hSignal,
hPtyReference,hConPtyProcess} struct it actually points to (layout
confirmed against microsoft/terminal's src/winconpty/winconpty.h).
Verified empirically with a standalone windows-sys scratch harness
(build 26200 / Windows 11 24H2+): duplicating exactly those 3 handles
plus the input/output pipe handles into a second process and
reconstructing this struct there produces a live HPCON that
ResizePseudoConsole accepts and that the running shell honors, even
after the original owner process exits -- confirmed via a real resize
round-trip (mode con: 80x25 -> 90x28) and independently via the
XTWINOPS `ESC[8;28;90t` sequence conhost itself emitted after the
resize. This held across all owner teardown orders tested: exiting
without ever calling ClosePseudoConsole, calling it explicitly, and
tearing down immediately without waiting for the successor's ack.

The hand-off path is designed to never call ClosePseudoConsole at all,
on any Windows version -- this sidesteps the documented pre-Windows-11-
24H2 blocking-wait behavior of that function entirely (untested on
that range of builds) rather than relying on empirically-observed
post-24H2 safety. into_handoff / into_handoff_handles use
std::mem::forget to suppress the normal Close-on-drop path; only the
caller's own now-transferred-out handle copies are ever closed.

This is additive: nothing about the existing spawn/read/write/resize
path changes, and nothing calls the new methods unless tanuki's own
Windows handoff code (not part of this vendored crate) does.

Vendored base: portable-pty 0.9.0
